  ($qb$S02C05Q01$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$The general level of trust employees in your organisation have in their immediate manager is:$qb$, $qb$High — most employees trust their manager to act in their interest and be honest with them$qb$, $qb$Moderate — trust is present but conditional; it has been tested by past experiences$qb$, $qb$Low — a significant portion of employees do not trust their immediate manager$qb$, $qb$Very low — distrust between employees and managers is a dominant feature of this organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C05Q02$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$When an employee shares a personal concern or sensitive information with their manager in your organisation, they can expect:$qb$, $qb$Confidentiality and appropriate support — managers are trusted with sensitive information$qb$, $qb$General discretion — information is typically handled with care but employees have some caution$qb$, $qb$Uncertainty — employees are not confident their manager will handle sensitive information appropriately$qb$, $qb$Risk — sharing sensitive information with a manager in this organisation is known to backfire$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C05Q03$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Employee trust in management in your organisation has, over the last 2 years:$qb$, $qb$Increased — specific actions or initiatives have built trust meaningfully$qb$, $qb$Remained stable — no significant trust-building or trust-damaging events have occurred$qb$, $qb$Declined — specific incidents or patterns have eroded trust$qb$, $qb$Significantly declined — a trust crisis between management and employees exists$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C05Q04$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$Employees in your organisation believe that management makes decisions with employee welfare in mind:$qb$, $qb$Yes — management is seen as genuinely considering employee impact in its decisions$qb$, $qb$Sometimes — employee welfare is considered but is not the primary driver of management decisions$qb$, $qb$Rarely — employee welfare considerations are often sacrificed to other priorities$qb$, $qb$No — employees believe management decisions are made without meaningful consideration of their welfare$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C05Q05$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$When management in your organisation makes a mistake that affects employees, the response is:$qb$, $qb$Acknowledged and repaired — management admits the error and takes steps to address the impact on employees$qb$, $qb$Acknowledged but not repaired — management admits the error but does not address consequences for employees$qb$, $qb$Minimised — mistakes affecting employees are acknowledged only under pressure$qb$, $qb$Denied — management mistakes that affect employees are not acknowledged$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C05Q06$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$E$qb$, $qb$Employees in your organisation feel they can approach management with problems without fear of negative consequences:$qb$, $qb$Yes — approaching management with problems is seen as professional and is responded to constructively$qb$, $qb$Mostly — employees generally feel safe raising problems but some managers are more approachable than others$qb$, $qb$Selectively — employees carefully choose what they raise and who they raise it with$qb$, $qb$No — approaching management with problems is seen as risky and is avoided$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C05Q07$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Management in your organisation follows through on commitments made to employees about working conditions, development, and recognition:$qb$, $qb$Reliably — employees trust that management commitments will be honoured$qb$, $qb$Mostly — major commitments are kept; smaller ones sometimes slip$qb$, $qb$Inconsistently — a pattern of unmet commitments has created scepticism$qb$, $qb$Rarely — employees have learned not to count on management commitments$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C05Q08$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$The relationship between employees and their direct managers in your organisation is best described as:$qb$, $qb$Productive and trusting — characterised by mutual respect, honest communication, and shared goals$qb$, $qb$Professional and adequate — functional but not deeply trusting$qb$, $qb$Transactional — limited to task-related communication; little trust or relationship investment$qb$, $qb$Strained — characterised by low trust, limited communication, or active tension$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C05Q09$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's investment in building management-employee trust (listening programmes, feedback mechanisms, management training) is:$qb$, $qb$Significant and genuine — trust-building is treated as a strategic priority$qb$, $qb$Moderate — some investment is made but it is not a consistent priority$qb$, $qb$Minimal — trust is expected to develop on its own without specific investment$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — management-employee trust is not managed as an organisational priority$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
