  ($qb$S04C05Q01$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Mechanisms for employees to raise concerns, suggestions, and feedback in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Multiple, accessible, and effective — employees have several channels and trust that input leads to action$qb$, $qb$Available but limited — one or two channels exist but their effectiveness is inconsistent$qb$, $qb$Nominally available — mechanisms exist but are not trusted or widely used$qb$, $qb$Absent — employees have no effective channels to raise concerns or suggestions$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C05Q02$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$E$qb$, $qb$Employees in your organisation feel that their input and suggestions are taken seriously by management:$qb$, $qb$Yes — employee suggestions regularly lead to visible changes and management credits the source$qb$, $qb$Sometimes — some suggestions are acted on; many are not$qb$, $qb$Rarely — employees have largely given up expecting their suggestions to lead to change$qb$, $qb$Never — the experience of having suggestions ignored has created widespread silence$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C05Q03$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Employee engagement surveys in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Regular, anonymous, and action-driving — results are shared and specific actions are named$qb$, $qb$Conducted but partially actioned — results are shared but action is inconsistent$qb$, $qb$Conducted but performative — surveys are run but results do not drive meaningful change$qb$, $qb$Non-existent or suppressed — employee engagement is not measured or results are managed rather than acted on$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C05Q04$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$Employees in your organisation feel they have genuine influence over how their work is done:$qb$, $qb$Yes — employees have real autonomy in how they approach their work within defined outcomes$qb$, $qb$Some — influence exists in some dimensions but is limited in others$qb$, $qb$Little — most work is prescribed with limited employee discretion$qb$, $qb$None — employees execute instructions with no meaningful influence over approach or method$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C05Q05$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Employee representative bodies, unions, or works councils in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Constructive and effective — employee representation is a genuine channel for voice and is taken seriously$qb$, $qb$Not applicable to our organisation — unions or works councils are not legally required or applicable in our operating context$qb$, $qb$Marginalised — representation exists but is not treated as a meaningful voice$qb$, $qb$Absent or suppressed — employee collective voice is not supported$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C05Q06$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$E$qb$, $qb$When employees in your organisation raise safety, ethics, or operational concerns, they are:$qb$, $qb$Heard and protected — concern-raising is safe and leads to genuine investigation$qb$, $qb$Generally heard — concerns are taken to some degree but the process is imperfect$qb$, $qb$Managed — concerns are acknowledged in a way that protects the organisation more than the employee$qb$, $qb$Punished — raising concerns in this organisation is known to damage the individual$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C05Q07$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Participative decision-making — involving employees in decisions that affect them — in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$A structured practice — relevant employees are routinely involved in decisions within their domain$qb$, $qb$Occasional — major decisions involve some employee input; routine ones do not$qb$, $qb$Rare — decisions are made at the top and communicated down$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — employee participation in decision-making is not a feature of this organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C05Q08$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$MM$qb$, $qb$Team meetings in your organisation give all team members a genuine opportunity to contribute:$qb$, $qb$Yes — meetings are facilitated to draw out all voices, not just the loudest$qb$, $qb$Mostly — most team members contribute; some voices are consistently marginalised$qb$, $qb$Partially — meetings are dominated by a few; many attend without contributing$qb$, $qb$No — team meetings are essentially broadcast sessions with no real two-way participation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C05Q09$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's response to negative employee feedback at an aggregate level (from surveys, exit interviews, informal sources) is:$qb$, $qb$Transparent and action-focused — leadership acknowledges negative patterns and names specific responses$qb$, $qb$Acknowledged — negative feedback is noted and some response is communicated$qb$, $qb$Managed — negative feedback is framed in the most positive light and action is limited$qb$, $qb$Suppressed — negative feedback at aggregate level is not shared or is actively managed down$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C05Q10$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The overall sense among employees that their voice matters in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — employees genuinely feel they can influence things that matter to them$qb$, $qb$Moderate — some influence is possible but employees do not feel their voice significantly matters$qb$, $qb$Weak — most employees feel their voice has no real impact$qb$, $qb$Very poor — powerlessness and voicelessness are defining employee experiences$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C06Q01$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The quality of working relationships between colleagues in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — collaborative, supportive, and characterised by mutual respect$qb$, $qb$Adequate — professional and functional but not particularly close or supportive$qb$, $qb$Variable — some teams have strong relationships; others are fractured or tense$qb$, $qb$Poor — interpersonal tension, competition, or disrespect is a significant feature of workplace relationships$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C06Q02$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$E$qb$, $qb$Employees in your organisation feel a sense of belonging and community at work:$qb$, $qb$Yes — most employees feel they are part of something, not just employed by something$qb$, $qb$Partially — belonging is experienced in teams but not across the organisation$qb$, $qb$Weakly — most employees feel professionally connected but not socially embedded$qb$, $qb$No — isolation and disconnection are common employee experiences$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C06Q03$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Diversity of background, experience, and thinking in teams in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Actively built — teams are deliberately composed for diversity of perspective and this is valued$qb$, $qb$Present but unmanaged — diversity exists but is not deliberately leveraged$qb$, $qb$Limited — teams tend to be homogeneous in background and thinking$qb$, $qb$Very limited — demographic and cognitive homogeneity is a significant organisational limitation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C06Q04$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$Social connection opportunities at work (team events, shared spaces, informal interaction) in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Regular and valued — the organisation invests in creating social connection as a deliberate priority$qb$, $qb$Occasional — some social activities exist but are not a consistent feature$qb$, $qb$Rare — social connection is left to individuals; the organisation does not invest in it$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the work environment actively discourages social connection$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C06Q05$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Remote or hybrid working in your organisation has:$qb$, $qb$Been managed well — connection, collaboration, and culture have been maintained or adapted effectively$qb$, $qb$Not applicable to our organisation — remote or hybrid working does not apply — all employees work on-site$qb$, $qb$Created significant disconnection — team cohesion and culture have deteriorated$qb$, $qb$Severely damaged team cohesion — isolation and disconnection are widespread consequences$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C06Q06$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$MM$qb$, $qb$The team dynamics within most teams in your organisation are characterised by:$qb$, $qb$Trust, collaboration, and psychological safety — teams work well together and support each other$qb$, $qb$Professional cooperation — teams function adequately but lack deep trust or mutual support$qb$, $qb$Transactional interaction — team members cooperate minimally around shared tasks$qb$, $qb$Dysfunction — competition, mistrust, or conflict characterise team dynamics$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C06Q07$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$E$qb$, $qb$New employees in your organisation feel welcomed and integrated into the team and culture:$qb$, $qb$Yes — onboarding is warm, thorough, and social integration is actively facilitated$qb$, $qb$Generally — new employees are welcomed but integration is informal and inconsistent$qb$, $qb$Poorly — new employees feel left to find their own way$qb$, $qb$Very poorly — poor onboarding and social integration is a known driver of early attrition$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C06Q08$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Cross-functional relationships between different departments in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Strong — people know and respect each other across functions; collaboration is natural$qb$, $qb$Adequate — cross-functional relationships are professional though not particularly close$qb$, $qb$Weak — silos mean most employees have limited knowledge of or connection with other departments$qb$, $qb$Hostile — cross-functional tension and competition is a dominant organisational feature$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C06Q09$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Investment in team building and social cohesion in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Deliberate and proportionate — team building is a regular practice with measurable impact on cohesion$qb$, $qb$Occasional — some investment is made but not systematically$qb$, $qb$Minimal — team building happens rarely and mostly in response to visible dysfunction$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — no investment is made in team cohesion beyond the work itself$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C06Q10$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Loneliness and isolation as employee experiences in your organisation (particularly in remote or distributed teams) are:$qb$, $qb$Actively managed — the organisation identifies and addresses isolation risk as a management responsibility$qb$, $qb$Acknowledged — isolation risk is recognised and some measures are in place$qb$, $qb$Undermanaged — isolation exists but is not seen as a management problem to solve$qb$, $qb$Severe and unaddressed — significant employee isolation is a known and growing problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C07Q01$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Employees in your organisation feel they have genuine opportunities for career advancement:$qb$, $qb$Yes — career paths are visible, fair, and most employees who perform have real advancement opportunities$qb$, $qb$Partially — advancement is possible for some but the process is not transparent or consistently fair$qb$, $qb$Limited — career advancement in this organisation is slow, opaque, or relationship-dependent$qb$, $qb$Very limited — the absence of career advancement opportunity is a primary driver of attrition$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C07Q02$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$E$qb$, $qb$Employees in your organisation feel their skills are growing and they are becoming more capable over time:$qb$, $qb$Yes — most employees can point to meaningful growth in capability since joining$qb$, $qb$Partially — some employees grow; others feel stagnant$qb$, $qb$Rarely — skill growth is not a common employee experience$qb$, $qb$No — employees leave this organisation with fewer marketable skills than they arrived with$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C07Q03$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Learning and development investment in your organisation (budget, time, programmes) is:$qb$, $qb$Significant and strategic — L&D is a competitive differentiator and a retention tool$qb$, $qb$Adequate — investment exists and is valued by employees$qb$, $qb$Minimal — L&D budget is among the first to be cut and is not a strategic priority$qb$, $qb$Negligible — there is effectively no meaningful L&D investment$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C07Q04$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$MM$qb$, $qb$Managers in your organisation have development conversations with their team members:$qb$, $qb$Regularly — development is discussed at 1:1s, not just performance review time$qb$, $qb$At appraisal time — development is discussed annually but not as a continuous conversation$qb$, $qb$Rarely — most managers do not discuss development unless the employee raises it$qb$, $qb$Never — development conversations between managers and employees do not happen$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C07Q05$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Internal mobility (moving between roles, departments, or locations) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Encouraged and supported — internal moves are facilitated and seen as organisational strength$qb$, $qb$Possible — internal mobility happens but is not actively facilitated$qb$, $qb$Difficult — internal mobility is limited by departmental protectionism or policy$qb$, $qb$Effectively blocked — employees who want to move internally typically leave the organisation instead$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C07Q06$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$E$qb$, $qb$The skills and capabilities employees develop in your organisation make them more valuable in the broader job market:$qb$, $qb$Significantly — working here builds highly transferable skills that are valued externally$qb$, $qb$Partially — some skills developed here are transferable; others are highly specific to the organisation$qb$, $qb$Minimally — the work here does not significantly develop externally valued capability$qb$, $qb$Not at all or negatively — working here does not build career capital$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C07Q07$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Stretch assignments, high-profile projects, and cross-functional opportunities are distributed in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Broadly and fairly — opportunities are accessible to high performers across functions and levels$qb$, $qb$Partially — some employees get regular opportunity; many do not$qb$, $qb$Narrowly — the same small group receives most development opportunities$qb$, $qb$Through relationships — development opportunity allocation is driven by proximity to power, not merit$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C07Q08$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$When an employee in your organisation is ready for promotion, the time between readiness and outcome is:$qb$, $qb$Reasonable — promotion happens within a clear timeframe once readiness is established$qb$, $qb$Somewhat long — promotions happen but the process is slower than employees feel is fair$qb$, $qb$Very long — employees wait years past readiness for promotion$qb$, $qb$Indefinite — promotion timelines are unpredictable and often do not materialise$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C07Q09$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's track record of developing leaders internally vs. consistently hiring externally for senior roles is:$qb$, $qb$Strong internal development — most senior roles are filled by people who grew within the organisation$qb$, $qb$Balanced — a mix of internal promotion and external hiring$qb$, $qb$External-dependent — most senior hires come from outside, limiting internal career horizons$qb$, $qb$Entirely external — internal career paths to seniority are effectively non-existent$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C07Q10$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The absence of career growth opportunity in your organisation contributes to attrition:$qb$, $qb$Minimally — employees feel they have sufficient growth opportunity and this is not a driver of departure$qb$, $qb$Somewhat — it is a factor for some employees but not the dominant one$qb$, $qb$Significantly — career stagnation is a frequently cited reason for departure$qb$, $qb$Critically — lack of career growth is the primary driver of talent loss in this organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C08Q01$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The onboarding experience for new employees in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Structured, warm, and effective — new joiners understand the culture, their role, and are productive quickly$qb$, $qb$Adequate — onboarding covers the basics but is not differentiated or particularly engaging$qb$, $qb$Minimal — onboarding is primarily administrative with limited cultural or professional integration$qb$, $qb$Poor — new employees are expected to figure things out for themselves with minimal support$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C08Q02$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$New employees in your organisation feel productive and confident in their role within:$qb$, $qb$The first 30–60 days — onboarding effectively accelerates productivity$qb$, $qb$60–90 days — integration happens but takes longer than it should$qb$, $qb$3–6 months — slow integration means new hires take a long time to become effective$qb$, $qb$Over 6 months or never — many new employees never feel fully integrated$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C08Q03$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Onboarding in your organisation includes meaningful cultural immersion:$qb$, $qb$Yes — new employees experience and understand the real culture through structured activities and leadership engagement$qb$, $qb$Partially — some cultural orientation is provided but it is not deep or experiential$qb$, $qb$Minimally — onboarding is procedural with limited cultural content$qb$, $qb$No — new employees discover the culture entirely through experience, without guided introduction$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
