  ($qb$S10C06Q04$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the tracking of market trends and industry shifts is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — market trends are systematically monitored and feed directly into strategic planning$qb$, $qb$Adequate — market trends are followed informally through industry media and network conversations$qb$, $qb$Weak — market trends are acknowledged but not systematically tracked or integrated into planning$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the organisation does not systematically monitor market trends$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C06Q05$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the win/loss analysis from competitive sales situations is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — win/loss data is collected, analysed for pattern, and shared with product and strategy teams$qb$, $qb$Adequate — win/loss analysis is conducted for major deals$qb$, $qb$Weak — win/loss data is collected but rarely analysed systematically$qb$, $qb$Very poor — win/loss analysis does not exist$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C06Q06$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the use of analyst and industry research is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — relevant analyst research (Gartner, Forrester, industry reports) is actively used in strategy$qb$, $qb$Adequate — some analyst research is accessed and used periodically$qb$, $qb$Weak — analyst research is accessed occasionally when the leadership is prompted$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the organisation does not use external analyst or industry research$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C06Q07$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the customer advisory boards or structured customer input into strategy is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — a formal customer advisory board or equivalent provides regular strategic input$qb$, $qb$Adequate — some structured customer input into strategy exists but is not formalised$qb$, $qb$Weak — customer input into strategy is informal and occasional$qb$, $qb$Very poor — customer strategic input does not exist$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C06Q08$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the social listening and brand monitoring is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — social listening tools track brand mentions, competitor activity, and market sentiment in real time$qb$, $qb$Adequate — some social listening is in place but not comprehensive$qb$, $qb$Weak — social listening happens reactively when a specific concern emerges$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the organisation does not monitor social or digital channels for market intelligence$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C06Q09$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the depth of competitor product and capability tracking is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — competitor products, features, and capabilities are regularly reviewed and compared to the organisation's own$qb$, $qb$Adequate — competitor products are tracked at a high level by the sales team$qb$, $qb$Weak — competitor product tracking is shallow and ad hoc$qb$, $qb$Very poor — competitor products are not systematically tracked$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C06Q10$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the overall market intelligence capability is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — the organisation has deep, current market intelligence that provides a genuine strategic advantage$qb$, $qb$Adequate — market intelligence is adequate for current strategic needs$qb$, $qb$Weak — market intelligence is shallow and limits strategic decision quality$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the organisation is operating with poor market intelligence$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C07Q01$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the perception of the organisation among key investors or funders is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — investors have strong confidence in leadership, strategy, and trajectory$qb$, $qb$Adequate — investor relations are well-managed and investors are broadly supportive$qb$, $qb$Weak — investors are adequate but not enthusiastic about the organisation's direction$qb$, $qb$Very poor — investor confidence in the organisation is low or declining$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C07Q02$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the perception among strategic partners is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — partners value the relationship and actively invest in and expand it$qb$, $qb$Adequate — partner relationships are professional and mutually respectful$qb$, $qb$Weak — partner relationships are transactional without deep mutual investment$qb$, $qb$Very poor — partner confidence is low or partner relationships are strained$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C07Q03$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the reputation among regulators or government bodies is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — regulators see the organisation as trustworthy, compliant, and cooperative$qb$, $qb$Adequate — the organisation has a positive regulatory relationship with no significant concerns$qb$, $qb$Weak — no significant regulatory concerns exist but the relationship lacks strength$qb$, $qb$Very poor — regulatory relationships are strained or under active scrutiny$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C07Q04$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the industry association membership and active participation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — the organisation is active in key associations generating visibility and credibility$qb$, $qb$Adequate — memberships exist but engagement is limited$qb$, $qb$Weak — memberships are held without meaningful engagement$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the organisation does not participate in industry associations$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C07Q05$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the relationship with community and social stakeholders is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — the organisation is a respected and engaged community contributor$qb$, $qb$Adequate — the organisation is seen as a responsible neighbour and employer$qb$, $qb$Weak — the organisation has no particular positive or negative community profile$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the organisation has a poor community or social reputation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C07Q06$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the overall brand perception among talent community is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — the organisation is known as a great place to build a career and actively attracts talent$qb$, $qb$Adequate — the talent community views the organisation favourably$qb$, $qb$Weak — the organisation is not particularly known in the talent community$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the organisation has a poor reputation as an employer in the talent community$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C07Q07$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the perception among key influencers (analysts, consultants, media) is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — key influencers are engaged regularly and are positive about the organisation$qb$, $qb$Adequate — some influencer relationships are maintained with reasonable perception$qb$, $qb$Weak — influencer engagement is ad hoc and limited$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the organisation does not actively manage influencer relationships$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C07Q08$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the supplier and vendor perception of the organisation as a partner is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — suppliers value the relationship and prioritise the organisation's needs$qb$, $qb$Adequate — supplier relationships are professional and mutually respectful$qb$, $qb$Weak — supplier relationships are transactional with limited partnership depth$qb$, $qb$Very poor — poor supplier relationships create supply chain risk$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C07Q09$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the overall stakeholder perception across all key groups is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — strong stakeholder relationships across all groups support the organisation's strategy$qb$, $qb$Adequate — stakeholder relationships are managed adequately without exceptional strength$qb$, $qb$Weak — notable stakeholder relationship gaps create strategic vulnerability$qb$, $qb$Very poor — weak or damaged stakeholder relationships are creating significant strategic risk$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C07Q10$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the quality of stakeholder communication and engagement is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — stakeholders receive timely, transparent, and relevant communication that builds trust$qb$, $qb$Adequate — stakeholder communication is adequate though not always timely or complete$qb$, $qb$Weak — stakeholder communication is inconsistent and leaves gaps that create concern$qb$, $qb$Very poor — stakeholder communication is poor and is actively eroding confidence in the organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C08Q01$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the crisis communication planning and readiness is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — a crisis communication plan exists, is tested, and senior leaders know their roles$qb$, $qb$Adequate — a basic plan exists but has not been tested or practised$qb$, $qb$Weak — the organisation knows it needs a crisis plan but does not have one$qb$, $qb$Very poor — there is no crisis communication preparation whatsoever$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C08Q02$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the track record of managing public reputational crises is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — past crises have been managed well and reputation was preserved or fully recovered$qb$, $qb$Adequate — crises have been managed without major lasting damage$qb$, $qb$Weak — some crises have been well-managed; others have caused lasting reputational damage$qb$, $qb$Very poor — past crises have damaged reputation in ways that have not been recovered$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C08Q03$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the real-time monitoring of brand threats is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — brand monitoring tools track online sentiment, competitor activity, and adverse media in real time$qb$, $qb$Adequate — monitoring occurs but not in real time — issues are often identified too late$qb$, $qb$Weak — monitoring happens only when an issue becomes publicly visible$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the organisation does not monitor for brand threats$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C08Q04$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the leadership communication capability during reputational challenges is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — leaders are trained and communicate clearly, calmly, and credibly under pressure$qb$, $qb$Adequate — leaders manage communications reasonably well in challenging situations$qb$, $qb$Weak — some leaders communicate well under pressure; others worsen reputational damage$qb$, $qb$Very poor — leadership communication in difficult situations tends to amplify rather than resolve damage$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C08Q05$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the proactive management of reputational risk is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — the organisation actively identifies and mitigates reputation risks before they materialise$qb$, $qb$Adequate — risk management processes address most reputation risks proactively$qb$, $qb$Weak — reputation risks are typically addressed only after they materialise$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the organisation takes limited proactive steps to prevent reputational damage$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C08Q06$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the cybersecurity incident management as a reputational risk is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — cybersecurity is treated as a reputation risk with robust incident response plans$qb$, $qb$Adequate — cybersecurity awareness and basic incident response capability exist$qb$, $qb$Weak — cybersecurity risk to reputation has not been fully assessed or planned for$qb$, $qb$Very poor — cybersecurity as a reputation risk is not on the agenda$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C08Q07$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the supplier and partner conduct risk management is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — supplier conduct is reviewed as part of due diligence and monitored ongoing$qb$, $qb$Adequate — major suppliers are screened but the process is not comprehensive$qb$, $qb$Weak — supplier conduct risk is acknowledged but not systematically managed$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the organisation does not consider supplier conduct as a reputation risk$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C08Q08$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the legal and compliance risk as a brand threat is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — legal and compliance issues are assessed for brand impact before they escalate$qb$, $qb$Adequate — legal issues are managed by legal without explicit brand impact consideration$qb$, $qb$Weak — brand impact of legal issues is considered only when they become public$qb$, $qb$Very poor — legal and compliance risks are not assessed for their brand impact$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C08Q09$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the social media risk management is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — policies and monitoring are in place for employee and organisational social media conduct$qb$, $qb$Adequate — a social media policy exists but enforcement is inconsistent$qb$, $qb$Weak — some guidance exists but social media risk is not actively managed$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the organisation has no social media risk management$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C08Q10$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the overall crisis management and reputation risk capability is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — the organisation is prepared for and resilient against reputational threats — a genuine strength$qb$, $qb$Adequate — basic crisis management capability exists and would be adequate for most scenarios$qb$, $qb$Weak — reputation risk management gaps create material vulnerability$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the organisation is significantly exposed to reputational risk with inadequate preparation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C09Q01$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the consistency and quality of customer experience is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — customers consistently describe the experience as better than expected — it is a brand differentiator$qb$, $qb$Adequate — customers are generally satisfied with the experience they receive$qb$, $qb$Weak — experience quality varies significantly depending on the team, channel, or timing$qb$, $qb$Very poor — a significant proportion of customers have negative experiences that damage the brand$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C09Q02$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the quality of the first impression experience is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — first impressions consistently reflect the best of the brand — new customers are impressed$qb$, $qb$Adequate — first impressions are positive and professional$qb$, $qb$Weak — first impressions are acceptable but not particularly compelling$qb$, $qb$Very poor — first impressions frequently fail to reflect the quality of the organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C09Q03$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the recovery when things go wrong is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — problems are resolved quickly, generously, and in a way that often increases customer loyalty$qb$, $qb$Adequate — problems are resolved professionally and fairly$qb$, $qb$Weak — problems are resolved but without the care to turn failures into positive experiences$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the organisation's response to service failures often makes the situation worse$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C09Q04$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the personalisation of the customer experience is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — customers feel known and valued as individuals — personalisation is a genuine brand differentiator$qb$, $qb$Adequate — some personalisation exists for key customers and segments$qb$, $qb$Weak — the customer experience is largely standardised without meaningful personalisation$qb$, $qb$Very poor — all customers receive the same impersonal experience regardless of their value or history$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C09Q05$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the emotional quality of customer interactions is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — interactions consistently leave customers feeling valued, confident, and satisfied$qb$, $qb$Adequate — most interactions are pleasant and professional$qb$, $qb$Weak — interactions are professional but rarely leave a positive emotional impression$qb$, $qb$Very poor — customers frequently feel frustrated, dismissed, or undervalued during interactions$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C09Q06$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the proactive post-purchase or post-service follow-up is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — the organisation proactively checks in with customers after delivery as standard practice$qb$, $qb$Adequate — some follow-up occurs for major engagements$qb$, $qb$Weak — post-delivery contact is rare unless the customer initiates$qb$, $qb$Very poor — no post-delivery follow-up is standard practice$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C09Q07$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the deliberate design of the customer journey is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — customer journeys are mapped, measured, and deliberately improved — a genuine practice$qb$, $qb$Adequate — some journey mapping has been done and improvements are partially systematic$qb$, $qb$Weak — the customer experience is managed without explicit journey design$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the customer experience has not been deliberately designed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C09Q08$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the measurement of customer experience quality is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — multiple measures are used regularly and improvements are systematically tracked$qb$, $qb$Adequate — some measurement occurs but not comprehensively or frequently enough$qb$, $qb$Weak — only basic satisfaction data is occasionally collected$qb$, $qb$Very poor — customer experience quality is not systematically measured$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C09Q09$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the overall customer experience as a brand driver is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — positive experiences generate advocacy and loyalty — CX is a powerful brand driver$qb$, $qb$Adequate — experiences are satisfactory without being consistently memorable$qb$, $qb$Weak — variable experiences undermine brand trust and consistency$qb$, $qb$Very poor — poor experiences are harming brand reputation and driving customer churn$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C09Q10$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the investment in customer experience improvement is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — structured investment in CX improvement delivers measurable improvements in NPS and loyalty$qb$, $qb$Adequate — some CX investment is made and improvements are visible$qb$, $qb$Weak — CX investment is minimal and improvement is slow$qb$, $qb$Very poor — CX is not invested in as a strategic brand driver$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C10Q01$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the long-term brand strategy (where the brand should be in 5 years) is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — a clear long-term brand strategy exists and current investment is actively building toward it$qb$, $qb$Adequate — a vision exists but daily activity is not clearly connected to long-term brand positioning$qb$, $qb$Weak — there is a general sense of direction but not a clear brand strategy$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the organisation does not have a long-term brand strategy$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C10Q02$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the brand evolution management is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — the brand is reviewed and evolved regularly to stay relevant as markets and audiences change$qb$, $qb$Adequate — brand evolution is considered during strategic planning cycles$qb$, $qb$Weak — the brand has not been meaningfully revisited in several years$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the brand is treated as fixed regardless of market change$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C10Q03$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the understanding of how target customers' needs are evolving is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — ongoing research provides deep insight into changing customer expectations and preferences$qb$, $qb$Adequate — customer changes are monitored through sales and service interactions$qb$, $qb$Weak — customer evolution is acknowledged but not systematically tracked$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the organisation has poor insight into how its customers and markets are changing$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C10Q04$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the investment in future brand capabilities is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — the organisation is deliberately building the brand assets it will need in 3-5 years$qb$, $qb$Adequate — some future-oriented brand investment is in place$qb$, $qb$Weak — brand investment is focused entirely on current needs without future planning$qb$, $qb$Very poor — there is no investment in building future brand capabilities$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C10Q05$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the ability to adapt brand messaging to different segments is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — segment-specific messaging is developed and deployed effectively across all key audiences$qb$, $qb$Adequate — some segmentation of messaging exists but it is not comprehensive$qb$, $qb$Weak — messaging is largely one-size-fits-all regardless of the audience$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the organisation uses a single message for all audiences$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C10Q06$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the sustainability, ESG, and responsible business as part of the brand is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — sustainability credentials are a genuine, credible, and communicated part of the brand$qb$, $qb$Adequate — ESG commitments exist but are not yet fully integrated into brand communication$qb$, $qb$Weak — sustainability claims are made without deep organisational commitment$qb$, $qb$Very poor — sustainability is not part of the brand strategy$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C10Q07$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the scenario planning for brand threats is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — brand threat scenarios are actively considered in strategic planning and mitigation actions are defined$qb$, $qb$Adequate — some scenario thinking is done in major planning cycles$qb$, $qb$Weak — brand threat planning happens only in response to visible threats$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the organisation does not plan for brand threats$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C10Q08$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the readiness to respond to disruptive brand threats is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — monitoring, response protocols, and team roles are all in place and have been tested$qb$, $qb$Adequate — basic readiness exists but protocols are not well-tested$qb$, $qb$Weak — some awareness exists but the organisation is not well-prepared$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the organisation would struggle to respond effectively to a major brand threat$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C10Q09$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the building brand presence in new markets or segments is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — specific brand-building activity is targeted at new markets as part of the growth strategy$qb$, $qb$Adequate — presence in new segments is growing organically without specific brand investment$qb$, $qb$Weak — the brand has limited presence in new segments being targeted for growth$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the organisation has not begun building brand in target new markets$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S10C10Q10$qb$, $qb$S10$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$In your organisation, the overall brand strategy and future positioning is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — the brand is being built deliberately for the future — a source of long-term competitive advantage$qb$, $qb$Adequate — future brand thinking is in progress and investment is growing$qb$, $qb$Weak — the brand follows rather than leads the market — reactive positioning$qb$, $qb$Very poor — the organisation is not investing in its future brand position$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true)
INSERT INTO question_bank (question_id, section_code, category_code, level_tag, text, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, score_a, score_b, score_c, score_d, active) VALUES
  ($qb$S11C01Q01$qb$, $qb$S11$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The senior leadership team in your organisation functions as:$qb$, $qb$A genuine team — shared goals, mutual accountability, and real collaboration on critical decisions$qb$, $qb$A functional group — generally aligned without deep collaboration$qb$, $qb$A set of parallel leaders — each focused on their domain with limited collective leadership$qb$, $qb$A dysfunctional group — leadership team tensions undermine organisational effectiveness$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S11C01Q02$qb$, $qb$S11$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Decision-making at leadership team level is:$qb$, $qb$Timely and inclusive — decisions are made with the right people involved and implemented quickly$qb$, $qb$Generally effective — decisions are made without major dysfunction$qb$, $qb$Slow or dominated — either prolonged or controlled by one person$qb$, $qb$Dysfunctional — the leadership team regularly fails to make or implement decisions effectively$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
