  ($qb$S16C09Q01$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Tolerance for bullying, intimidation, or harassment in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Zero — any such behaviour is immediately addressed regardless of the perpetrator's seniority$qb$, $qb$Low — such behaviour is generally not tolerated and is addressed when identified$qb$, $qb$Variable — tolerance depends on the seniority of the perpetrator$qb$, $qb$High — bullying or intimidating behaviour by senior people is tolerated$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C09Q02$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Chronic underperformance that is consistently not addressed in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Absent — performance standards are upheld across all levels$qb$, $qb$Rare — underperformance is generally addressed in a timely way$qb$, $qb$Present — some chronic underperformance exists in teams or at certain levels without being addressed$qb$, $qb$Widespread — the organisation tolerates systematic underperformance that is well-known and demoralising$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C09Q03$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Favouritism (unearned preferential treatment) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Absent — decisions about people are visibly merit-based$qb$, $qb$Occasional — isolated cases exist but it is not a systemic cultural feature$qb$, $qb$Significant — favouritism is a known feature of the culture in some parts of the organisation$qb$, $qb$Pervasive — favouritism is widely experienced as a primary driver of decisions and advancement$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C09Q04$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The experience of cliques or in-groups that exclude others in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Absent — the culture is inclusive and no groups feel systematically excluded$qb$, $qb$Rare — some informal groupings exist but they do not meaningfully exclude$qb$, $qb$Present — cliques are a recognisable cultural feature that affects inclusion and advancement$qb$, $qb$Dominant — clique dynamics significantly determine who gets access, support, and advancement$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C09Q05$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The presence of cynicism or disengagement as a cultural norm in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Rare — the culture is energetic and optimistic$qb$, $qb$Limited — some cynicism exists but it is not pervasive$qb$, $qb$Moderate — significant cynicism about leadership, strategy, or the future exists$qb$, $qb$High — pervasive cynicism is one of the most prominent cultural features of the organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C09Q06$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Gaslighting (dismissing legitimate employee concerns as misunderstandings) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Absent — employee concerns are taken seriously and investigated honestly$qb$, $qb$Rare — occasional dismissive responses to concerns exist but are not systemic$qb$, $qb$Occasional — some employees experience their concerns being dismissed without genuine investigation$qb$, $qb$Common — a significant proportion of employees have experienced their legitimate concerns being dismissed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C09Q07$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Systematic overwork or exploitation of employee goodwill in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Absent — the organisation manages workload sustainably and respects employee boundaries$qb$, $qb$Occasional — peak periods require extra effort but recovery time is provided$qb$, $qb$Regular — sustained overwork is the norm for significant parts of the organisation$qb$, $qb$Severe — systematic exploitation of employee effort is a defining feature of the culture$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C09Q08$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The organisation's handling of complaints and grievances in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Fair, prompt, and independent — complaints are taken seriously and investigated thoroughly$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — most complaints are handled professionally$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — some complaints are handled well; others are not taken seriously$qb$, $qb$Poor — complaints are systematically minimised, discouraged, or handled in ways that protect the accused$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C09Q09$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The willingness of leadership to acknowledge and address cultural dysfunction in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — leadership actively seeks to identify and address cultural problems$qb$, $qb$Generally present — leadership responds when cultural problems are clearly evidenced$qb$, $qb$Low — leadership tends to minimise or deny the severity of cultural problems$qb$, $qb$Very low — leadership consistently denies or dismisses cultural dysfunction$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C09Q10$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Overall, the level of cultural toxicity and dysfunction in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is very low — this is a genuinely healthy organisation$qb$, $qb$Is low — minor cultural issues exist without being significant$qb$, $qb$Is moderate — there are areas of genuine dysfunction that need to be addressed$qb$, $qb$Is high — significant cultural toxicity is one of the most serious challenges facing the organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C10Q01$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Measurement of organisational culture in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Regular and multi-dimensional — culture is measured through surveys, focus groups, and exit data with results reported to leadership$qb$, $qb$Periodic — culture is measured through an annual engagement survey$qb$, $qb$Occasional — culture surveys happen but not on a regular schedule$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation does not systematically measure its culture$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C10Q02$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Employee engagement survey quality in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — surveys are well-designed, ask the right questions, and produce actionable insight$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — surveys produce useful data$qb$, $qb$Limited — surveys are too basic to produce meaningful insight$qb$, $qb$Poor — surveys are a tick-box exercise that does not produce genuine cultural understanding$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C10Q03$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The action taken on culture and engagement survey findings in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Structured and transparent — findings are shared broadly and specific action plans are created and tracked$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — action is taken on major findings$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — some findings lead to action; others are acknowledged and then ignored$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — surveys are conducted without meaningful follow-up action$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C10Q04$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Follow-through on culture improvement commitments in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Reliable — when the organisation commits to cultural improvements, they are implemented$qb$, $qb$Generally reliable — most commitments are followed through$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — some culture commitments are fulfilled; others are not$qb$, $qb$Poor — culture improvement commitments are frequently not followed through undermining trust$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C10Q05$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Qualitative culture insight (beyond surveys — listening sessions, focus groups, exit data analysis) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Regularly used — qualitative methods are used alongside surveys for a richer picture$qb$, $qb$Occasionally used — some qualitative methods supplement survey data$qb$, $qb$Rarely used — the organisation relies primarily on survey data without deeper qualitative insight$qb$, $qb$Not used — the organisation does not gather qualitative culture insight$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C10Q06$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Manager accountability for culture within their teams in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Clear — managers are held accountable for the culture within their teams through measurement and review$qb$, $qb$Generally present — managers are broadly accountable$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — accountability for culture varies significantly by manager and level$qb$, $qb$Absent — managers are not held accountable for the culture within their teams$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C10Q07$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Culture change programme quality in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — when culture change is initiated, it is structured, resourced, and sustained$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — culture change programmes are reasonably designed$qb$, $qb$Variable — culture change quality varies significantly between initiatives$qb$, $qb$Poor — culture change programmes are typically under-resourced and fail to achieve their objectives$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C10Q08$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The use of external benchmarks to understand the quality of the culture in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Regular — the organisation benchmarks its culture data against industry peers$qb$, $qb$Occasional — benchmarking occurs periodically$qb$, $qb$Rare — the organisation rarely benchmarks its culture externally$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation does not benchmark its culture against external data$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C10Q09$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The integration of culture health into leadership and executive decision-making in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — culture data is a regular input into leadership decisions$qb$, $qb$Generally present — culture is considered in major leadership decisions$qb$, $qb$Limited — culture is considered reactively when a specific problem forces it$qb$, $qb$Absent — culture health is not systematically integrated into leadership decision-making$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C10Q10$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Overall, the quality of culture measurement and improvement in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine capability — the organisation understands its culture and is systematically improving it$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — the organisation measures culture and acts on the findings$qb$, $qb$Has notable gaps — culture measurement and improvement is not robust enough$qb$, $qb$Is insufficient — the organisation has poor insight into its culture and is not effectively improving it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true)
