  ($qb$S18C08Q01$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The perceived fairness of how decisions are made in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — employees broadly believe decisions are made fairly and with good intent$qb$, $qb$Generally present — most employees feel decisions are reasonable$qb$, $qb$Mixed — some decisions are perceived as fair; others create significant fairness concerns$qb$, $qb$Low — widespread perception of unfairness is a significant cultural and morale problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C08Q02$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The experience of inclusion (all employees feeling they genuinely belong) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — inclusion is consistently experienced across all groups$qb$, $qb$Generally positive — most employees feel included$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — some groups feel more included than others$qb$, $qb$Poor — meaningful groups of employees feel excluded or marginalised$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C08Q03$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The degree to which employees feel they can be their authentic self at work in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — the culture actively encourages authenticity and people do not feel they need to hide aspects of themselves$qb$, $qb$Generally positive — most employees feel they can be reasonably authentic$qb$, $qb$Variable — some employees feel free to be authentic; others feel they must conform$qb$, $qb$Low — most employees feel they need to mask aspects of themselves to fit in$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C08Q04$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The experience of diverse perspectives being valued in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — diverse thinking is actively sought and different perspectives are genuinely valued$qb$, $qb$Generally positive — diversity of thought is broadly welcomed$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — diverse perspectives are valued in some contexts and dismissed in others$qb$, $qb$Poor — the culture is conformist and different perspectives are not genuinely valued$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C08Q05$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The degree to which all employees regardless of background have equal opportunity in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — opportunity is genuinely equal and the organisation actively ensures this$qb$, $qb$Generally present — equal opportunity is broadly maintained$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — some groups experience significantly different opportunities$qb$, $qb$Poor — unequal opportunity is a known and unaddressed cultural problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C08Q06$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The allyship culture (employees actively supporting inclusion of colleagues from different backgrounds) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — employees are active allies who speak up for inclusion$qb$, $qb$Generally present — most employees are supportive of colleagues from different backgrounds$qb$, $qb$Weak — allyship is occasional rather than a cultural norm$qb$, $qb$Very weak — most employees are passive and exclusionary behaviour goes unchallenged$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C08Q07$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The organisation's response when discrimination or exclusion is reported in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Fast and decisive — reports are taken seriously and addressed promptly$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — reports are addressed professionally$qb$, $qb$Slow — discrimination and exclusion complaints take too long to be resolved$qb$, $qb$Poor — discrimination and exclusion are not consistently or adequately addressed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C08Q08$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The quality of role models for underrepresented groups at senior levels in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — diverse senior role models are visible and active in sponsoring others$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — some diversity is visible at senior levels$qb$, $qb$Limited — underrepresented groups have few senior role models within the organisation$qb$, $qb$Very limited — the absence of senior diversity is a known barrier to inclusion$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C08Q09$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The celebration of diversity (recognising different backgrounds, perspectives, and contributions) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Active and genuine — diversity is authentically celebrated as a strength$qb$, $qb$Present — some diversity celebration exists$qb$, $qb$Token — diversity is mentioned but celebration feels performative$qb$, $qb$Absent — diversity is not celebrated or is actively avoided as a topic$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C08Q10$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Overall, fairness, inclusion, and belonging in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine competitive strength — the culture makes everyone feel welcome and valued$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — inclusion is broadly present$qb$, $qb$Has notable gaps — inclusion deficits are creating meaningful engagement and retention problems for specific groups$qb$, $qb$Is a serious concern — poor inclusion and belonging are significantly limiting the organisation's culture and performance$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C09Q01$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The frequency and quality of performance feedback in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — employees receive regular, specific, and actionable feedback$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — feedback is provided though not always with sufficient frequency or depth$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — feedback quality varies significantly across managers$qb$, $qb$Poor — most employees receive feedback too infrequently or in ways that are not useful$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C09Q02$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The willingness of managers to give honest, developmental feedback in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — managers provide honest, caring feedback as a matter of course$qb$, $qb$Generally present — most managers give adequate feedback$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — some managers give great feedback; others avoid difficult feedback conversations$qb$, $qb$Low — avoidance of honest feedback is a widespread management pattern$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C09Q03$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The receptiveness of employees to receiving feedback in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — employees actively seek feedback and respond constructively$qb$, $qb$Generally positive — most employees receive feedback reasonably well$qb$, $qb$Variable — some employees are receptive; others are defensive$qb$, $qb$Low — defensive or negative reactions to feedback are common$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C09Q04$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$360-degree feedback or peer feedback practices in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Well-established and valued — multi-directional feedback is embedded in the development culture$qb$, $qb$Present — some 360 or peer feedback practice exists$qb$, $qb$Limited — multi-directional feedback is used only in specific contexts$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — feedback flows only downward from managers to employees$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C09Q05$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The quality of formal performance review processes in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — performance reviews are well-designed, well-facilitated, and produce genuine development insight$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — performance reviews serve a useful purpose$qb$, $qb$Variable — quality of performance review conversations varies significantly$qb$, $qb$Poor — performance reviews are widely experienced as formulaic, stressful, or useless$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C09Q06$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The connection between performance feedback and reward in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Clear — feedback directly informs reward decisions in a transparent and credible way$qb$, $qb$Generally present — feedback influences reward though the connection is not always transparent$qb$, $qb$Weak — feedback and reward decisions are often disconnected$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — there is no clear connection between performance feedback and reward outcomes$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C09Q07$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The culture of continuous improvement in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — employees at all levels are always looking for better ways to work$qb$, $qb$Generally present — continuous improvement is a valued cultural attribute$qb$, $qb$Patchy — some teams continuously improve; others maintain the status quo$qb$, $qb$Weak — the culture is comfortable with the status quo and does not value continuous improvement$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C09Q08$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The psychological safety to give upward feedback (employees giving feedback to their managers) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — upward feedback is actively sought and safely given$qb$, $qb$Generally present — most employees feel they can give reasonable feedback upward$qb$, $qb$Low — most employees are cautious about giving feedback to their managers$qb$, $qb$Very low — giving feedback to a manager carries significant perceived risk$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C09Q09$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The degree to which feedback leads to visible behaviour change in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — feedback is demonstrably acted on and this reinforces the feedback culture$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — feedback produces some behaviour change$qb$, $qb$Limited — feedback is received but seldom produces visible change$qb$, $qb$Very limited — feedback does not lead to change and this is demotivating for those giving it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C09Q10$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Overall, the feedback culture and continuous improvement orientation in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine competitive advantage — the organisation learns and improves faster than peers$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — feedback and improvement culture are reasonably strong$qb$, $qb$Has notable gaps — feedback culture weaknesses are limiting development and performance$qb$, $qb$Is a serious concern — poor feedback culture is significantly constraining learning and performance$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C10Q01$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The primary drivers of employee motivation in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Well-understood — leadership has a nuanced, data-driven understanding of what motivates employees$qb$, $qb$Generally understood — the main motivational drivers are known$qb$, $qb$Partially understood — some motivational drivers are known but the picture is incomplete$qb$, $qb$Not understood — the organisation does not have a clear picture of what motivates its employees$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C10Q02$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The alignment between what the organisation offers and what motivates its employees in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — the employee experience is well-designed around genuine motivational drivers$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — the main motivational needs are met$qb$, $qb$Partial — some motivational needs are well-met; others are not adequately addressed$qb$, $qb$Weak — significant misalignment between what the organisation offers and what motivates employees$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C10Q03$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The ability of the organisation to tailor the employee experience to individual motivational needs in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — managers actively personalise the work experience for individuals$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — some personalisation of the employee experience occurs$qb$, $qb$Limited — the employee experience is largely standardised without meaningful personalisation$qb$, $qb$Very limited — the organisation offers the same experience to everyone regardless of individual needs$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C10Q04$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The degree to which employees feel the organisation genuinely cares about their wellbeing and career in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — most employees feel genuinely cared for as people, not just as resources$qb$, $qb$Generally present — the organisation demonstrates adequate care for employees$qb$, $qb$Variable — some employees feel genuinely cared for; others do not$qb$, $qb$Low — most employees feel the organisation treats them primarily as a resource rather than as people$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C10Q05$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The gap between what the organisation promises (EVP) and what employees experience in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Minimal — the reality of working here closely matches the promise$qb$, $qb$Small — minor gaps exist but do not significantly affect trust or motivation$qb$, $qb$Significant — there are notable gaps between promise and reality that affect engagement$qb$, $qb$Very large — the EVP and lived experience are so different that it is causing significant disillusionment$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C10Q06$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The quality of exit conversations to understand what would have kept people in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — exit conversations produce deep insight that drives retention improvements$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — useful information is gathered$qb$, $qb$Limited — exit conversations are superficial and produce little actionable insight$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation does not have substantive exit conversations$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C10Q07$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The degree to which the organisation adjusts based on what employees tell it is not working in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — employee input demonstrably influences how the organisation operates$qb$, $qb$Generally present — significant employee concerns are addressed$qb$, $qb$Limited — employee feedback is heard but rarely leads to meaningful change$qb$, $qb$Very limited — employees do not believe their feedback makes any difference$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C10Q08$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The sense that the organisation is investing in employees' futures in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — employees feel the organisation is genuinely invested in their long-term development$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — employees feel the organisation cares about their development$qb$, $qb$Weak — employees feel the organisation takes more than it gives in the employment relationship$qb$, $qb$Very weak — employees feel exploited rather than developed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C10Q09$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The degree to which employees feel positive about remaining with the organisation long-term in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — most employees intend to stay and see a future here$qb$, $qb$Generally positive — more employees intend to stay than to leave$qb$, $qb$Mixed — similar numbers intend to stay and leave$qb$, $qb$Low — most employees do not see a long-term future at the organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C10Q10$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Overall, the talent motivation and retention environment in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine competitive strength — the organisation is an employer of choice$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — the organisation retains and motivates most of the talent it needs$qb$, $qb$Has notable gaps — motivational and retention deficits are creating talent risk$qb$, $qb$Is a serious concern — poor motivation and retention are threatening the organisation's talent base$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true)
INSERT INTO question_bank (question_id, section_code, category_code, level_tag, text, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, score_a, score_b, score_c, score_d, active) VALUES
  ($qb$S19C01Q01$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's customer experience (CX) strategy is:$qb$, $qb$Clearly defined, communicated, and actively used to guide investment and improvement$qb$, $qb$Generally defined — a CX direction exists and is broadly followed$qb$, $qb$Vague — the organisation talks about customer experience without a clear strategy$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — customer experience is managed without a defined strategy$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C01Q02$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Investment in customer experience improvement in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strategic and sustained — CX investment is a defined budget line with measurable outcomes$qb$, $qb$Growing — investment in CX is increasing$qb$, $qb$Limited — some CX investment occurs but it is not comprehensive or sustained$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation does not invest specifically in CX improvement$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C01Q03$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The definition of the target customer experience (what the organisation is trying to achieve for customers) is:$qb$, $qb$Precise and shared — all customer-facing employees know exactly what experience they are trying to deliver$qb$, $qb$Generally understood — the target CX is broadly communicated$qb$, $qb$Vague — the target experience is described in general terms without clarity$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation has not defined what customer experience it is trying to deliver$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C01Q04$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Accountability for the overall customer experience in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Clear — a specific leader or function owns the end-to-end customer experience$qb$, $qb$Generally clear — customer experience ownership is understood$qb$, $qb$Shared but diffuse — multiple functions share responsibility without a clear overall owner$qb$, $qb$Absent — no one is clearly accountable for the overall customer experience$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C01Q05$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Customer experience measurement and reporting to senior leadership in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Regular and comprehensive — CX metrics are on the senior leadership dashboard$qb$, $qb$Periodic — CX performance is reported to leadership periodically$qb$, $qb$Occasional — CX performance reaches leadership only when it becomes a problem$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — senior leadership does not receive regular customer experience reporting$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C01Q06$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's use of customer experience as a competitive differentiator is:$qb$, $qb$Active — CX is explicitly positioned as a competitive advantage$qb$, $qb$Developing — CX is increasingly used as a differentiator$qb$, $qb$Limited — CX is not systematically used as a competitive positioning tool$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation does not position customer experience as a differentiator$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C01Q07$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Customer experience design (deliberate design of customer journeys and touchpoints) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Sophisticated — customer journeys are mapped, designed, and regularly improved$qb$, $qb$Developing — some journey mapping and design is in place$qb$, $qb$Basic — some journey thinking has been done without systematic design$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — customer experience has not been deliberately designed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C01Q08$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The integration of CX across all customer-facing functions (sales, service, delivery, billing) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Seamless — all customer-facing functions deliver a consistent and coordinated experience$qb$, $qb$Generally coordinated — most handoffs and touchpoints are managed$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — some touchpoints are excellent; others create friction$qb$, $qb$Poor — fragmented CX delivery creates significant inconsistency for customers$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C01Q09$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's use of customer journey mapping in its management practices is:$qb$, $qb$Active — journeys are mapped, used in management decisions, and regularly updated$qb$, $qb$Periodic — journey mapping has been done and is used in some contexts$qb$, $qb$Limited — some journey mapping work has been done without sustained use$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — customer journey mapping is not used$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C01Q10$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Overall, the customer experience strategy in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine competitive advantage — the organisation is a CX leader in its market$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — CX strategy provides sufficient direction$qb$, $qb$Has notable gaps — CX strategy weaknesses are limiting the quality of the customer experience$qb$, $qb$Is a serious concern — absence of CX strategy is resulting in poor and inconsistent customer experience$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C02Q01$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The consistency of service quality across all customer touchpoints and channels in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — customers receive a consistently high-quality experience regardless of channel or person$qb$, $qb$Generally consistent — service quality is broadly maintained$qb$, $qb$Variable — quality varies significantly between channels, teams, and individuals$qb$, $qb$Very inconsistent — service quality is highly variable and customers experience significant differences$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C02Q02$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The speed of service delivery in your organisation meets customer expectations:$qb$, $qb$Consistently — the organisation reliably delivers within or ahead of committed timescales$qb$, $qb$Generally — most service is delivered on time$qb$, $qb$Inconsistently — on-time delivery varies significantly$qb$, $qb$Poorly — late delivery is a frequent customer complaint$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C02Q03$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The quality of the end product or service delivered to customers in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — customers consistently receive what they expected at the quality level committed$qb$, $qb$Good — quality is generally high with occasional exceptions$qb$, $qb$Variable — quality is good at times and below standard at others$qb$, $qb$Poor — quality problems are a frequent source of customer dissatisfaction$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C02Q04$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Service delivery processes in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Well-designed, documented, and consistently followed — processes enable reliable, high-quality delivery$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — processes are in place and broadly followed$qb$, $qb$Inconsistently followed — processes exist but individual variation is high$qb$, $qb$Poorly designed or not followed — service delivery quality depends on individual rather than process$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C02Q05$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The accuracy of information provided to customers during service delivery in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — customers receive accurate, complete information at all stages$qb$, $qb$Generally accurate — most information is correct$qb$, $qb$Variable — accuracy depends on the person and channel$qb$, $qb$Poor — inaccurate information is a frequent cause of customer dissatisfaction$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C02Q06$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The proactiveness of communication to customers during delivery (keeping them informed) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — customers are proactively updated at all key stages without needing to ask$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — customers receive adequate communication during delivery$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — proactive communication varies significantly by team or channel$qb$, $qb$Poor — customers regularly have to chase for updates$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C02Q07$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The degree to which front-line employees take personal ownership of customer outcomes in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — front-line employees act as owners of the customer experience$qb$, $qb$Generally present — most front-line employees take reasonable ownership$qb$, $qb$Variable — ownership quality varies significantly across individuals$qb$, $qb$Low — most front-line employees focus on completing tasks rather than owning customer outcomes$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C02Q08$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Service capacity and resource allocation to meet customer demand in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Well-managed — service capacity is proactively managed to meet demand without compromising quality$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — capacity is broadly matched to demand$qb$, $qb$Sometimes insufficient — capacity constraints occasionally degrade service quality$qb$, $qb$Often insufficient — capacity problems are a frequent cause of poor service delivery$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C02Q09$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Error rate and defect rate in service delivery in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Low and declining — quality management processes minimise errors and the trend is improving$qb$, $qb$Acceptable — error rates are within industry norms$qb$, $qb$High — errors are more frequent than they should be$qb$, $qb$Very high — errors and defects are a major and unresolved service quality problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
